ncept': 'pandemic', 'relevance': 0.3240673099}\n {'concept': 'seafood', 'relevance': 0.295488563}\n {'concept': 'impact', 'relevance': 0.2471085427}\n {'concept': 'partnership', 'relevance': 0.2368417188}\n {'concept': 'consumers', 'relevance': 0.1869636762}\n {'concept': 'business', 'relevance': 0.0424911659}]", 'category_bra': None, 'category_for': None, 'category_hra': None, 'category_hrcs_hc': None, 'category_hrcs_rac': None, 'category_icrp_cso': None, 'category_icrp_ct': None, 'category_rcdc': None, 'category_sdg': None, 'category_uoa': None, 'categories': {'bra_v1': None, 'for_v1': None, 'hra_v1': None, 'hrcs_hc_v1': None, 'hrcs_rac_v1': None, 'icrp_cso_v1': None, 'icrp_ct_v1': None, 'rcdc_v1': None, 'sdg_v1': None, 'uoa_v1': None, 'bra_v2020': None, 'for_v2020': {'first_level': {'codes': "['11']", 'full': "[{'id': '2211', 'code': '11', 'name': 'Medical and Health Sciences'}]"}, 'second_level': {'codes': "['1117']", 'full': "[{'id': '3177', 'code': '1117', 'name': 'Public Health and Health Services'}]"}}, 'hrcs_hc_v2020': None, 'hrcs_rac_v2020': None, 'icrp_cso_v2020': None, 'icrp_ct_v2020': None, 'rcdc_v2020': None, 'for_2020_v2022': None}, 'date_inserted': '2021-08-17 08:28:37+00:00', 'date_imported_gbq': '2022-08-31 03:03:16+00:00', 'resulting_publication_ids': '[]', 'funder_org_acronyms': "['NOAA']"}, {'id': 'grant.9747434', 'title': 'Using Observations of Gaseous Compounds in the LA Basin during COVID-19 to Elucidate Sources and Atmospheric Processes Affecting Urban Air Quality', 'abstract': 'The PIs propose to build on a collaborative effort initiated in March 2020, to collect in-situ data in Pasadena, CA within the South Coast Air Basin. A suite of gas- and particle-phase instrumentation was co-located on the Caltech campus and samples were collected from April- August 2020. The major activities proposed here are collection of a complementary data set from April-August 2022; analysis and modeling of the 2020 and 2022 data sets; and data sharing and reporting for 2020 and 2022. The objectives of the proposed research are to: 1) identify and quantify the ambient gas-phase organic compounds (intermediate and volatile organic compounds, I/VOCs) across a gradient of human activity during and after the COVID-19 shelter in-place orders; 2) quantify I/VOC source markers and evaluate the contribution of sourcespecific emissions to measured I/VOCs; 3) compare calculated emission ratios to published emission ratios, to assess spatial and temporal changes in these values and to evaluate the localized impacts of reduced human activity during COVID-19 on atmospheric composition; and 4) assess the contribution of identified compounds to O3 and PM2.5 formation and the sensitivity of O3 and PM2.5 formation to observed changes in I/VOCs and meteorological parameters.', 'language': 'en', 'original_title': {'value': 'Using Observations of Gaseous Compounds in the LA Basin during COVID-19 to Elucidate Sources and Atmospheric Processes Affecting Urban Air Quality', 'language': 'en'}, 'original_abstract': {'value': 'The PIs propose to build on a collaborative effort initiated in March 2020, to collect in-situ data in Pasadena, CA within the South Coast Air Basin. A suite of gas- and particle-phase instrumentation was co-located on the Caltech campus and samples were collected from April- August 2020. The major activities proposed here are collection of a complementary data set from April-August 2022; analysis and modeling of the 2020 and 2022 data sets; and data sharing and reporting for 2020 and 2022. The objectives of the proposed research are to: 1) identify and quantify the ambient gas-phase organic compounds (intermediate and volatile organic compounds, I/VOCs) across a gradient of human activity during and after the COVID-19 shelter in-place orders; 2) quantify I/VOC source markers and evaluate the contribution of sourcespecific emissions to measured I/VOCs; 3) compare calculated emission ratios to published emission ratios, to assess spatial and temporal changes in these values and to evaluate the localized impacts of reduced human activity during COVID-19 on atmospheric composition; and 4) assess the contribution of identified compounds to O3 and PM2.5 formation and the sensitivity of O3 and PM2.5 formation to observed changes in I/VOCs and meteorological parameters.', 'language': 'en'}, 'linkout': None, 'grant_number': 'NA21OAR4310223', 'foa_number': 'Not available', 'start_date': '2021-09-01', 'end_date': '2023-08-31', 'start_year': 2021, 'end_year': 2023, 'active_years': '[2021 2022 2023]', 'investigators': '[]', 'researcher_ids': '[]', 'research_orgs': "['grid.266093.8']", 'organisation_details': "[{'name': 'UNIVERSITY OF CALIFORNIA, IRVINE', 'city': 'Irvine', 'country': 'US', 'grid_id': 'grid.266093.8'}]", 'research_org_cities': '[5359777]', 'research_org_state_codes': "['US-CA']", 'research_org_countries': "['US']", 'funder_org': 'grid.3532.7', 'funder_org_cities': '[4140963]', 'funder_org_state_codes': "['US-DC']", 'funder_org_countries': "['US']", 'funding_amount': 199852.0, 'funding_currency': 'USD', 'funding_aud': 273575.0, 'funding_cad': 252606.0, 'funding_chf': 185212.0, 'funding_eur': 178051.0, 'funding_gbp': 149576.0, 'funding_jpy': 23448372.0, 'funding_nzd': 293533.0, 'funding_usd': 199852.0, 'funding_cny': 1275818.0, 'concepts': "[{'concept': 'human activities', 'relevance': 0.7564126621}\n {'concept': 'South Coast Air Basin', 'relevance': 0.7481653841}\n {'concept': 'emission ratio', 'relevance': 0.7469586187}\n {'concept': 'gas-phase organic compounds', 'relevance': 0.7430201783}\n {'concept': 'sensitivity of O3', 'relevance': 0.7177968444}\n {'concept': 'LA Basin', 'relevance': 0.6906665195}\n {'concept': 'atmospheric processes', 'relevance': 0.6879780362}\n {'concept': 'atmospheric composition', 'relevance': 0.6868986245}\n {'concept': 'urban air quality', 'relevance': 0.6867373951}\n {'concept': 'situ data', 'relevance': 0.6834462115}\n {'concept': 'meteorological para